From: OpenHarmonyPCDeveloper <noreply@gitcode.com>
Subject: [PATCH] tcp.c: provide IPPORT_USERRESERVED when libc omits it

musl's <netinet/in.h> (the libc behind OpenHarmony) defines
IPPORT_RESERVED but dropped the historical IPPORT_USERRESERVED macro,
so tcp_lookup() fails to compile with

    tcp.c:121:19: error: use of undeclared identifier 'IPPORT_USERRESERVED'

BSD and glibc both define it as 1023 -- the last port below the reserved
range.  Add the value back behind a plain #ifndef guard so the guard is
driven by the libc's own macro set rather than by any platform test,
and so libcs that do provide the macro keep their own value.  Behaviour
is unchanged: the /etc/services name lookup stays restricted to ports
<= 1023.

--- a/tcp.c	2026-10-01 07:47:32.471356984 +0800
+++ b/tcp.c	2026-10-01 07:47:32.531017221 +0800
@@ -37,6 +37,16 @@
 #if HAVE_NETINET_TCP_H
 # include <netinet/tcp.h>
 #endif
+/* IPPORT_USERRESERVED is not provided by musl's <netinet/in.h> (it only keeps
+ * IPPORT_RESERVED).  BSD and glibc have always defined it as 1023, i.e. the
+ * highest port below the reserved range.  Supply the historical value when the
+ * C library omits the macro so that the getservbyport() lookup below stays
+ * bounded to ports <= 1023, exactly as upstream intends.
+ */
+#ifndef IPPORT_USERRESERVED
+# define IPPORT_USERRESERVED 1023
+#endif
+
 #if HAVE_ARPA_INET_H
 # include <arpa/inet.h>
 #endif
